## ===========================================================================
## DAME SANDLER AND THE PREPARATIONS — Book 12 Parts 1-2 (`story.ni:10588-
## 10966`) with the clean-Jack layers of Books 3 and 4 (`story.ni:5065-5194`
## the clothier, `5244-5267` Sandler's door, `5440-5602` the Chorus Brothers,
## `4703-4820` and `10905-10958` the armory), Chapter 9.
##
## The change document ("Chapter 9 — Dame Sandler and the Preparations",
## 2026-09-02) authorizes the chapter from Sandler's door to the moment
## Journey to the Ball begins: the reveal, the jewel, Pieter from guard to
## bodyguard, the loan, the gown, the dagger, and the step onto Commerce
## Street with all three. No new rooms — Chapter 2's shops in their clean-Jack
## state — so this file carries the chapter's OWN things (the bookkeeping
## entity, the four jewels, the purse and its coins, the ball gown, the plain
## dagger) and every phrase the chapter speaks; the clauses that must sit on
## Chapter 2's rooms and people are layered onto `lords-market.chord` and
## `commerce-street.chord`, gated on `the preparations`. Every line is
## Gentry's; conversations are STUBS under the standing rule.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 9):
##   * EXTENT — Sandler's door to evening on Commerce Street (David: "C");
##     the night journey is Chapter 10's, and the port's numbering runs one
##     past the source's from here. Chapter X's row begins when `the
##     preparations becomes journey`, set on Commerce Street with gown, jewel
##     and dagger in hand; the evening text, the hour turned to night and the
##     purse removed are Chapter 10's opening, not built here.
##   * SANDLER'S REVEAL — true words throughout, the public name unsaid
##     (David: "A"). DS31-49 carry as written for address and lineage; DS38
##     and DS39 CONVERT from the source's will-of-the-people law to §3e's
##     succession claim, in David's words (gap 1) — the stub marks them.
##   * WHO SEES HER — Pieter does NOT see (David: "A"); his lines carry as
##     written, "Jack" slips and the stammer included. Sandler sees. The
##     Brothers and the clothier do not; Olgan Minor does, and Pieter refuses
##     his conversation, so OM1-9 are never reached here.
##   * PRESSURE — quiet, as the source has it (David: "A"). Pieter's steering
##     refusals are the only pressure and they are about time.
##
## STUBS TAKING THE SOURCE'S PATH (flagged, per the standing rule's item 4):
## `talk to sandler` after DS31 prints the DS32-DS49 TODO in one turn and
## fires DS48's and DS49's consequences (the case unlocked and the four
## pieces laid out; she may leave once a jewel is taken), where the source
## walks the tree one quip at a time and fires DS49 by itself once the
## DS45-48 cluster is spent (the designers' note, `story.ni:10715`, carried
## below). `talk to brothers` clean prints the credentials TODO while they
## are contemptuous and the CB6 loan — the purse changing hands — once
## the letter has been shown; the letter shown restarts nothing (the source
## restarts on CB2/CB3). `talk to clothier` clean prints the CL1-6 TODO;
## CL6 (buying by conversation) is `buy clothes`. `talk to pieter` prints
## PI1-PI11; PI12 is the Woods' and Chapter 10's.
##
## GAPS (change document): the DS38-39 conversion (David's words); the
## dagger "under your gown" when bought before the gown (carries); CB7's
## "yesterday" (carries — the port's clock agrees); Pieter's ten road
## mutters, authorized here, heard in Chapter 10.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * "now the noun is worn" (the jewel, the gown, the dagger) — no
##     runtime statement puts a garment on (GH #360): each arrives in her
##     hands and `wear` is the player's; every gate reads `has`, as the
##     source's Journey trigger does. The dagger's "(being worn under your
##     ball gown)" inventory tail goes with it.
##   * Pieter's presence line as bodyguard — a presence line cannot vary
##     (GH #364), so his on-duty line and his five bodyguard lines are room
##     markers, and the platform lists him besides.
##   * `show dagger to X` / `give dagger to X` ("Best to keep it hidden") —
##     a showing clause's owner is the recipient, not the thing shown.
##   * `ask brothers for loan` (the persuasion rules) — no asking-for
##     action; `talk to brothers` is the way.
##   * `pull drawstring` opening the purse — a pulling clause cannot redirect
##     to opening; `open purse` and `close purse` speak the drawstring lines.
##   * The purse's conditional aliases ("coins", "gold" only while closed).
##   * `take clothes` as buying — an intercept with statements would run
##     under the platform's scenery refusal; `buy`, `pick` and `choose` are
##     the ways.
##   * The clothier's clean bell paragraph replaces nothing: the source
##     prints it on a `[first time]` of its own rule, so a clean first entry
##     squeals once whether or not the dirty bell rang before.
##   * `give letter to brothers` — a speaking give still hands the item
##     over (the Chapter 6 finding), so the brothers' `after` hands the
##     letter back; "They hand the letter back to you" is the source's own
##     line for it.
##   * `open purse` / `close purse` print the platform's report and then the
##     drawstring line; the source's line replaces the report.
##   * `kiss` refusals (no action); the source's image and theme changes.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE PREPARATIONS — the state the shops, the streets and Pieter read.
## `pending` until she enters Sandler's clean; `reveal` from DS31, held in
## the shop; `told` from DS49, the case open; `escorted` from DS49B, Pieter
## the bodyguard; `journey` on Commerce Street with all three, which is
## Chapter X's row; `ball` from the step into the ballroom, which ends the
## Bodyguard scene (Chapter 11).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the preparations
  aka dame sandler's errands, the errands
  proper
  states: pending, reveal, told, escorted, journey, ball

  Dame Sandler's preparations.

## "the player has dress jewelry" — any one of the four.

define condition jeweled: the player has the silver ring or the player has the silver pendant or the player has the silver bracelet or the player has the silver brooch

## The source's Bodyguard scene runs from DS49B to the ballroom
## (`story.ni:10758`): Pieter's steering and his shop manners hold through
## Chapter 10's journey, so the streets read this rather than `escorted`.

define condition bodyguarded: the preparations is escorted or the preparations is journey

