## `DS31` (`story.ni:10615`), on entering clean; the port's Chapter IX row
## is the source's "Chapter 9 - Preparations" heading (`5262`).

define phrase sandler-ds31-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — Dame Sandler looks you over and smiles, not in
  the least surprised. 'Lady Jacqueline,' she says, inclining her head, 'how
  nice to see you finally looking yourself, after all these years. Come,
  child, let me get a look at you.' She takes your chin between finger and
  thumb and turns your face to the light. 'You've simply destroyed your hair,
  of course.')
end phrase

## `DS32-DS49` (`story.ni:10617-10668`) as one stub: the reveal, the claim,
## the murder, the task, the three errands, the jewel, and "Thank you".
## DS38 and DS39 are marked for conversion (the ruling: the succession
## claim, David's words). The designers' note on DS49 (`story.ni:10715`):
## the "Thank you" quip fires by itself once the DS45-48 cluster is spent,
## because stopping her at the door only to be thanked "comes off as weird
## and passive aggressive" — the stub fires it in the same turn.

define phrase sandler-ds32-ds49-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — 'Did you really think I hadn't guessed?' says
  Dame Sandler. She has had her eye on you for years, she says, as have
  others; she and Fiona made their plans the week the Duke died. Fossville may
  well have murdered your father — poison seems most obvious — and the claim
  is yours by blood: the King's brother's child, and the King dead without a
  named heir. 'You must announce yourself. Fossville's ball is tonight. You
  will need a dress, some jewelry, and a weapon.' The clothier is next door;
  the weapons dealer is on Commerce Street; the Chorus Brothers will want
  proof of your heritage. She unlocks the display case with a tiny silver key.
  'Take any one you like, child. I'm one of those who would see you succeed;
  this is my gift to you.' When you thank her she is already moving you to the
  door. 'See the moneylenders first, then get a suitable dress and a discreet
  blade. Pieter will go with you—' 'What, to the ball?' 'Yes, Pieter, to the
  ball. Just do what you always do — keep your mouth shut and make sure no one
  harms the valuables.')
end phrase

## `story.ni:10594`

define phrase sandler-no-time
  Dame Sandler interrupts you with an upheld finger. 'There's no time,
  child. You must hurry.'
end phrase

## `story.ni:10600` — the source's line lacks its opening quotation mark;
## the port supplies it.

define phrase sandler-proof-of-heritage
  'That is proof of your heritage, Jacqueline,' Dame Sandler says. 'Guard it
  well; it is the most valuable thing you possess.'
end phrase

## `story.ni:10603`: held in the shop from DS31 to DS49. The source varies
## the line on DS44 — "'Wait, Jacqueline. There are a few things yet you
## should know.'" once the errands have been named — which the one-turn stub
## never reaches; the first variant is the one that can print.

define phrase sandler-dont-go-yet
  Dame Sandler takes your elbow, gently but firmly. 'Please don't go yet,
  dear; what I have to tell you is dreadfully important.'
end phrase

