    choose the target

## `story.ni:5111`

define phrase already-a-lovely-gown
  You are already wearing a perfectly lovely ball gown.
end phrase

## `story.ni:5114-5121`, the purchase and the fitting; Pieter waits outside
## and the day dress is gone from play (`5116`).

define phrase gown-bought
  After several agonizing minutes of indecision, you point to the dress you
  want, and hand over several coins.

  The clothier flutters his hands and practically giggles, 'Oh, a splendid
  choice! You'll look like a princess!' He dances around you, taking
  measurements with his cord and muttering numbers to himself as he goes.
  Then he takes the dress off its dummy and retreats with it into the back
  of his shop. 'This will be just a moment,' he calls over his shoulder.

  You look at Pieter and shrug.

  'Er, I'll wait outside,' he says.

  And afterwards, as you look at yourself in the full-length mirror while
  the clothier kneels at your feet, tugging, plucking, pinning, making
  adjustments, you realize that he was right — you do look like a princess.
end phrase

## `story.ni:5171-5173`

create the ball gown
  aka gown, dress, ball dress, pale blue dress, blue dress, pale blue gown, new dress, new gown, finery, new finery, pearls, tiny pearls, bodice, dagged sleeves, sleeves
  wearable

  The dress is pale blue with tiny pearls embroidered into the bodice and
  dagged sleeves.

  on the player taking_off
    refuse gown-not-the-time
  end on

define phrase gown-not-the-time
  This isn't the time or place to get undressed.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## OLGAN MINOR, WITH PIETER (`story.ni:10905-10958`): the proprietor finds
## something else to look at; the dagger under Pieter's glare.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## `story.ni:10913`, once.

define phrase minor-finds-something-else
  The proprietor makes a face as you walk into his shop, but when he sees
  Pieter behind you he quickly finds something else to look at.
end phrase

## `story.ni:10916`; the tail once.

define phrase minor-dont-waste-your-breath, stopping
  'Don't waste your breath making conversation, Jacqueline,' Pieter says.
  'Just pick something and buy it and let's move on.' You get the impression
  that Pieter really doesn't like Olgan Minor, and that maybe there's a
  history between the two.
or
  'Don't waste your breath making conversation, Jacqueline,' Pieter says.
  'Just pick something and buy it and let's move on.'
end phrase

## `story.ni:4730`, the dagger display touched with Pieter behind her.

define phrase minor-shut-your-trap
  'Touch one of those and-'

  'Shut your trap, Minor,' snaps Pieter, and Minor falls silent and even
  takes a step or two back. 'Hurry up and buy something, Jacqueline,' Pieter
  says.
end phrase

## `story.ni:10919`

define phrase pieter-too-big-to-wear
  Pieter shakes his head. 'Those sorts of weapons are too big to wear
  discreetly,' he tells you, 'and most are too heavy for you to lift in any
  case.'
end phrase

## `story.ni:10925-10927`, the purchase. "under your gown" carries whether
## or not the gown is bought yet (gap 2).

define phrase dagger-bought
  Pieter leans over your shoulder as you inspect the daggers. 'Having the
  right weapon is as important as knowing what to do with it,' he tells
  you. 'You don't want big or fancy; you want something sharp, long enough
  to do damage to a man when you stick him with it, but small enough to
  hide under your, er...' he suddenly blushes and clears his throat.
  'Anyway. That one,' he says, pointing to a plain-handled blade about six
  inches long. 'That's the one you want.'

  Olgan Minor grumbles, but Pieter glares at him again and suggests that if
  your gold is no good here, then perhaps some other metal might suit the
  shopkeeper better. Grudgingly, Minor hands over the knife, and makes a
  show of biting your gold coin to make sure of its worth.

  You slip the knife under your gown. Already you feel more confident.
end phrase

## THE PLAIN DAGGER (`story.ni:10931-10958`): worn under the gown, never
## drawn before the ball (Chapter 11's), never dropped.

create the plain dagger
  aka dagger, knife, blade, plain blade, plain-looking blade, plain-handled blade, weapon
  wearable

  It's a plain-looking blade about six inches long, easily concealed beneath
  your gown.

  on the player dropping
    refuse dagger-keep-it-close
  end on

  on the player taking_off
    refuse dagger-keep-it-hidden
  end on

## `draw [plain dagger]` is taking off (`story.ni:10945`).

extend action taking_off
  grammar
    draw the target

define phrase dagger-keep-it-close
  You may need that dagger — keep it close!
end phrase

define phrase dagger-keep-it-hidden
  Best to keep it hidden for now.
end phrase
